    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-70945-8",
    "abstract": "Global warming has profound effects on the terrestrial hydrological cycle, leading to alterations in regional extreme weather patterns. While terrestrial precipitation responses under continued greenhouse gas emissions are well established, the responses of terrestrial precipitation and vegetation feedbacks under climate mitigation scenarios remain uncertain. Here, we investigate terrestrial precipitation changes under idealized negative and zero CO<sub>2</sub> emissions scenarios using the Community Earth System Model version 2 (CESM2). Terrestrial precipitation increases by approximately 1.1% at the peak CO<sub>2</sub> concentration ( ~ 725 ppm), but shows even greater increases of about 1.9% and 2.5% under substantially lower atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub> concentrations following zero ( ~ 600 ppm) and negative ( ~ 430 ppm) CO<sub>2</sub> emissions, respectively. Our results suggest that enhanced transpiration from terrestrial vegetation largely contributes to this increase under the negative emissions scenario. Furthermore, despite a substantial increase in terrestrial precipitation, extreme precipitation events and droughts become less severe globally under the negative emissions scenario, even compared to the zero emissions scenario. While near-term mitigation is essential to curb immediate warming, these findings suggest that sustained negative emissions could be effective for achieving long-term reductions in hydrological extremes and enhancing terrestrial water availability.",
    "field": "Climate change mitigation",
    "problem": "The responses of terrestrial precipitation and vegetation feedbacks under climate mitigation scenarios involving zero and negative CO\u2082 emissions remain uncertain, complicating predictions of long-term changes to the land's hydrological cycle and extreme weather events.",
    "method": "This study utilizes the Community Earth System Model 2 (CESM2) to conduct idealized zero and negative CO2 emission-driven simulations, investigating the long-term response of the terrestrial hydrological cycle and the potential for mitigating hydrological extremes.",
    "task": "The task is to investigate terrestrial precipitation changes and hydrological extremes under idealized negative and zero CO\u2082 emission scenarios using the Community Earth System Model version 2 to assess long-term mitigation strategies.",
